            <ArticleTitle>Capecitabine-induced cardiotoxicity: when to suspect? How to manage? A case report.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>722-6</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.2459/JCM.0b013e32832bb9b1</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>We present a case of capecitabine-induced cardiac toxicity manifested by chest pain, ST-segment elevation and ventricular tachycardia. Symptoms and ECG alterations were completely reversible after withdrawal of the drug. Coronary angiography demonstrated the absence of epicardial coronary spasm. We suggest cardiac monitoring with ECG Holter and effort ECG during the first days of drug administration. Prompt evaluation of chest pain in this setting is of paramount importance.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
